WEBVTT

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:05.260
NoSQL injection is less about SQL syntax and more about unsafe query structure.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.740
In document databases, search systems, and JSON-driven APIs,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.820
user-controlled objects can sometimes influence operators, filters, selectors,

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.320
or nested conditions if they are passed directly into query construction.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.495
The risk is that the application accepts more structure or behavior than it intended.

00:00:20.495 --> 00:00:24.261
JavaScript developers should pay attention to JSON body parsing and object shape.

00:00:24.261 --> 00:00:29.195
A request body may look like ordinary data, but it can contain nested objects, arrays,

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:32.615
unexpected types, or fields that the route did not intend to expose.

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:36.955
If application code passes that structure directly into a query, update, or filter,

00:00:36.955 --> 00:00:39.221
the user may influence more than a simple value.

00:00:39.221 --> 00:00:41.687
Schema validation and type checking are important here.

00:00:41.687 --> 00:00:45.440
Define the expected shape of input, reject unknown fields where appropriate,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.335
constrain types, set length limits,

00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:50.520
and map accepted fields into application-owned query objects.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.520
This is different from simply checking that the request is valid JSON.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.315
JSON syntax only proves the input can be parsed,

00:00:56.315 --> 00:00:58.491
not that it represents an authorized query.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:02.408
Allowlisting fields and operators is especially important for filtering APIs.

00:01:02.408 --> 00:01:05.960
A client may be allowed to filter by status, owner, or creation date,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.200
but not by arbitrary internal fields or special query operators.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.140
The application should translate approved client choices into controlled query behavior

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.730
rather than treating the request body as the query itself.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.760
Mass assignment and over-posting are related concerns.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.090
If a JavaScript API accepts an entire object and writes or queries with it directly,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.215
clients may provide fields the developer did not intend to honor.

00:01:26.215 --> 00:01:29.800
The safer pattern is to validate intent, construct explicit objects,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.160
and make the allowed behavior visible in code.
